(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-medicine-and-surgery-i-exam-7-pulmonary-nodules-neoplasms-vignettes-version-2"]={"title":"Pulmonary Nodules and Neoplasms Vignettes 2 — CMS I Exam 7","category":"Clinical Medicine and Surgery I","exam":"Exam 7","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"A 72-year-old man smoked 1.5 packs a day for 30 years and quit 22 years ago. He has no symptoms and no known lung nodule, and he asks about lung cancer screening scans. Which describes his eligibility for screening?","choices":["Eligible for yearly screening","Eligible on pack-years alone","Not eligible: too old","Not eligible: quit too long ago"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Screening covers adults 55 to 80 with at least a 30 pack-year history who smoke now or quit within the last 15 years. He is 72 with 1.5 packs a day x 30 years = 45 pack-years, but he quit 22 years ago, beyond the 15-year window, so he does not qualify. — Pulmonary Nodules and Neoplasms 2026.pptx, Slides 3, 18"},{"q":"A 70-year-old man has a peripheral cavitating lung mass. Histology shows anaplastic, undifferentiated, pleomorphic cells, with no features of adenocarcinoma or squamous cell carcinoma and no specific marker. He asks how his cancer type was decided. Which response is accurate?","choices":["A specific marker confirmed it","Cavitation on imaging proved it","Sputum cytology identified it","It is a diagnosis of exclusion"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Large cell carcinoma lacks specific genetic or histologic markers and is a diagnosis of exclusion among the non-small cell cancers. Its cells are anaplastic, undifferentiated and pleomorphic, which separates it from adenocarcinoma and squamous cell carcinoma, and histology must confirm the anaplastic tissue even when computed tomography suggests it. — Pulmonary Nodules and Neoplasms 2026.pptx, Slides 33, 34, 39, 40, 41"},{"q":"A 58-year-old man has smoked 1 pack of cigarettes a day for 25 years. He plans to keep smoking but cut down to half a pack a day, and he asks whether the amount he smokes each day makes any difference to his lung cancer risk. Which response is accurate?","choices":["Cutting back removes his risk","Packs and years compound the risk","Years count, packs per day do not","His risk is already fixed"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Smoking is implicated in 85 to 90% of lung cancers, and both the packs smoked per day and the years of smoking compound the risk, raising it 5 times, then 10 times, then 20 times as exposure grows. His daily amount therefore matters, not just how long he has smoked. — Pulmonary Nodules and Neoplasms 2026.pptx, Slide 3"},{"q":"A 67-year-old woman smoked 2 packs a day for 20 years and quit 8 years ago. She has no cough, hemoptysis or weight loss, and no lung nodule has ever been found. She asks about lung cancer screening. Which is the most appropriate recommendation?","choices":["Annual low-dose computed tomography","No screening, since she quit","Yearly sputum cytology","Yearly standard-dose computed tomography"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Screening is recommended for high-risk adults without symptoms or a known nodule who are 55 to 80 years old, with at least a 30 pack-year history, who smoke now or quit within the last 15 years. She is 67, has 2 packs a day x 20 years = 40 pack-years, and quit 8 years ago, so she qualifies for an annual low-dose chest scan. — Pulmonary Nodules and Neoplasms 2026.pptx, Slides 3, 14, 18"},{"q":"A 71-year-old man with a 60 pack-year history has 2 months of cough, wheezing and blood-streaked sputum. Computed tomography shows a central mass arising in the right main bronchus, and sputum cytology shows malignant cells. Which lung cancer type is most likely?","choices":["Small cell carcinoma","Adenocarcinoma","Large cell carcinoma","Squamous cell carcinoma"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Squamous cell carcinoma sits centrally and often originates in a bronchus, has the strongest association with smoking, and causes cough, wheezing and hemoptysis once it obstructs the bronchus. Unlike the other lung cancers it can be caught with sputum testing, so malignant cells in his sputum from a central bronchial mass point to it. — Pulmonary Nodules and Neoplasms 2026.pptx, Slides 30, 33, 39, 40"},{"q":"A 67-year-old man has a biopsy-proven 2.5-cm non-small cell carcinoma in the left upper lobe. Positron emission tomography shows uptake in mediastinal and subcarinal lymph nodes on the same side as the tumor, and there is no distant metastasis. Which stage is his cancer?","choices":["Stage 2B","Stage 3A","Stage 3B","Stage 4A"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Mediastinal or subcarinal node involvement (N2) automatically moves a cancer to stage 3, and a tumor no more than 5 cm that has spread to the mediastinal and subcarinal nodes is stage 3A. His tumor is only 2.5 cm, but the same-side mediastinal nodes set the stage. — Pulmonary Nodules and Neoplasms 2026.pptx, Slides 22, 25, 26, 27, 29"},{"q":"A 47-year-old woman has months of episodes of skin flushing, watery diarrhea with cramps, and wheezing. Exam shows an enlarged liver and a heart murmur, and echocardiography shows thickening of the tricuspid and pulmonic valves. Computed tomography shows a mass in a bronchus. Which tumor is most likely?","choices":["Squamous cell carcinoma","Adenocarcinoma","Bronchial carcinoid","Small cell carcinoma"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Carcinoid syndrome, which in the lung arises mainly from a bronchial carcinoid, comes from release of serotonin by the tumor cells. Its features include cutaneous flushes, diarrhea, cramps, nausea and vomiting, cough, wheezing and dyspnea, hepatomegaly, and pulmonic and tricuspid valve thickening and stenosis, matching her picture. — Pulmonary Nodules and Neoplasms 2026.pptx, Slides 4, 31, 46, 48"},{"q":"A 58-year-old woman has a peripheral lung adenocarcinoma resected. The pathology report describes lepidic growth (tumor cells spreading along the alveolar lining). She asks which lung cells this cancer usually starts from. Which response is accurate?","choices":["Type I pneumocytes","Neuroendocrine cells","Cells lining the bronchus","Type II pneumocytes"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Type II pneumocytes are usually the origin of adenocarcinomas, starting with atypical adenomatous hyperplasia and growing first in situ, then enlarging by lepidic growth across the alveolar lining or invading the stroma. Type II pneumocytes are cuboidal, secrete surfactant and make up 60% of alveolar epithelial cells but only 3 to 5% of the surface. — Pulmonary Nodules and Neoplasms 2026.pptx, Slides 6, 8, 32, 39, 41"},{"q":"A 73-year-old man with chronic obstructive pulmonary disease and a 65 pack-year history has worsening cough, wheezing and hemoptysis. Computed tomography shows a central mass in a main bronchus with cavitation and a spiculated (spiky) border. Which noninvasive test can sometimes catch this tumor type, unlike the other lung cancers?","choices":["Sputum cytology","Serum calcium level","Mucicarmine stain","Chest radiograph"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. A central, cavitating bronchial mass in a heavy smoker with cough, wheezing and hemoptysis is the picture of squamous cell carcinoma, which unlike the other lung cancers can be caught with sputum testing. Sputum cytology is not sensitive in general, so tissue biopsy is still the standard. — Pulmonary Nodules and Neoplasms 2026.pptx, Slides 14, 30, 32, 39, 40, 46"},{"q":"A 66-year-old woman with a newly diagnosed lung cancer is meeting a radiation oncologist. Her son has read that some lung cancers respond far better to radiotherapy than others, and he asks which type benefits most. Which response is accurate?","choices":["Small cell carcinoma","Adenocarcinoma","Large cell carcinoma","Squamous cell carcinoma"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Small cell carcinoma responds better to chemotherapy and radiation than the three non-small cell cancers, and radiation works best when it is in limited stage. Non-small cell cancers do not respond well to chemotherapy or radiation, which serve as adjuncts to surgery or for stage 4. — Pulmonary Nodules and Neoplasms 2026.pptx, Slides 33, 35, 41"},{"q":"An 83-year-old woman who still smokes has a 60 pack-year history. She has no symptoms and no known lung nodule. Her daughter asks whether she should start yearly lung cancer screening scans. Which describes her eligibility?","choices":["Eligible every 5 years","Not eligible because of age","Eligible as a current smoker","Not eligible while she smokes"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Screening is recommended for adults 55 to 80 with at least a 30 pack-year history who smoke now or quit within the last 15 years. Her 60 pack-years and current smoking would qualify, but at 83 she is beyond the upper age limit of 80. — Pulmonary Nodules and Neoplasms 2026.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"A 62-year-old man with a 50 pack-year history and no cough or shortness of breath comes to the emergency department after a first seizure. He reports 3 weeks of morning headaches, vomiting and blurred vision, and fundoscopic exam shows papilledema. Chest computed tomography shows a 4-cm lung mass. Which site of spread is most likely?","choices":["Brain","Liver","Bone","Adrenal gland"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Patients may have no respiratory symptoms and present instead with complaints from metastasis. Brain metastasis causes headaches, vision changes, nausea, vomiting, syncope and seizures, and raised intracranial pressure shows as papilledema on fundoscopic exam, which is his exact picture beside a lung mass. — Pulmonary Nodules and Neoplasms 2026.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"A 45-year-old woman who has never smoked and has no family history of lung cancer has a solitary 4-mm solid nodule found on computed tomography done for abdominal pain. She asks whether she should be worried about it. Which response is accurate?","choices":["It needs a biopsy now","Size does not change concern","Its small size is reassuring","Most nodules are cancerous"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The first evaluation of a nodule is its size, and anything smaller than 6 mm is generally not concerning unless the patient is at high risk. Most nodules are benign, mainly granulomas or hamartomas. She has no smoking or family history, so her 4-mm nodule falls in the low-concern group. — Pulmonary Nodules and Neoplasms 2026.pptx, Slides 12, 16"},{"q":"A 67-year-old man with a 50 pack-year smoking history has a mass arising in a bronchus, and the biopsy shows squamous cell carcinoma. His oncologist explains that lung carcinomas are sorted into two main groups that are treated differently. Into which group does his cancer fall?","choices":["Non-small cell lung carcinoma","Small cell lung carcinoma","Carcinoid tumor","Mixed adenosquamous type"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Lung carcinomas fall into two main types: small cell lung carcinoma (oat cell) and non-small cell lung carcinoma, which is subdivided by histology into adenocarcinoma, squamous cell carcinoma and large cell carcinoma. His squamous cell carcinoma therefore belongs to the non-small cell group. — Pulmonary Nodules and Neoplasms 2026.pptx, Slides 4, 30"},{"q":"A 68-year-old man with known metastatic non-small cell lung carcinoma has 3 weeks of fatigue, weight loss and dizziness. His blood pressure is 88/56 mm Hg, serum sodium 128 mEq/L (normal 135-145) and serum potassium 5.9 mEq/L (normal 3.5-5.0). Which complication best explains these findings?","choices":["Brain metastasis","Liver metastasis","Adrenal metastasis","Inappropriate antidiuretic hormone"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Metastatic spread can cause adrenal insufficiency, with fatigue, weight loss, dizziness, hypotension and electrolyte imbalances: hyponatremia together with hyperkalemia. His low blood pressure, sodium of 128 against a floor of 135, and potassium of 5.9 against a ceiling of 5.0 complete that picture. — Pulmonary Nodules and Neoplasms 2026.pptx, Slides 13, 48"},{"q":"A 70-year-old man has a biopsy-proven 3-cm adenocarcinoma in the left lower lobe and a new left pleural effusion. Analysis of the fluid shows malignant cells. There is no spread outside the chest. Which stage is his cancer?","choices":["Stage 2B","Stage 3B","Stage 4A","Stage 4B"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. A malignant pleural effusion is scored M1a, distant metastasis, along with a tumor in the other lung or a pleural or pericardial nodule. Stage 4A covers spread into the other lung, the lung's lining, the heart's lining or the fluid around the lungs or heart, or a single site outside the chest. His malignant effusion makes him stage 4A whatever the tumor size. — Pulmonary Nodules and Neoplasms 2026.pptx, Slides 22, 25, 29"},{"q":"A 60-year-old man with a 40 pack-year history has a concerning 2-cm peripheral nodule on computed tomography. He is afraid of needles and asks whether a sputum test could rule out cancer instead of a biopsy. Which response is accurate?","choices":["A negative result rules it out","It works best for peripheral tumors","It can stage the cancer","It is not a sensitive test"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Sputum cytology is listed in the workup but is not sensitive, so a negative result cannot rule out cancer. It is mainly useful for squamous cell carcinoma, a central bronchial tumor that can be caught with sputum testing, while peripheral tumors such as adenocarcinoma and large cell carcinoma are not. Biopsy classes the tumor type. — Pulmonary Nodules and Neoplasms 2026.pptx, Slides 14, 22, 30, 40"},{"q":"A 65-year-old woman with lung adenocarcinoma diagnosed 6 months ago has sudden, severe mid-back pain that began during a coughing fit. She has not fallen. Imaging shows a fresh vertebral fracture. Which complication best explains the fracture?","choices":["Liver metastasis","Adrenal metastasis","Bone metastasis","Brain metastasis"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Lung cancer spreads to bone, which can be anywhere, including the spine. Bone metastasis causes bone pain, fractures without a fall, even a spinal fracture from coughing, and spinal cord compression with neurologic dysfunction. A vertebral fracture from a cough in a patient with lung adenocarcinoma fits exactly. — Pulmonary Nodules and Neoplasms 2026.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"A 68-year-old man who still smokes and has a 70 pack-year history has 2 months of cough, wheezing and hemoptysis. Computed tomography shows a large, cavitated central mass arising from a bronchus. A metabolic panel shows a serum calcium of 12.8 mg/dL (normal 8.5-10.5). Which lung cancer type is most likely?","choices":["Adenocarcinoma","Squamous cell carcinoma","Small cell carcinoma","Large cell carcinoma"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Squamous cell carcinoma has the strongest association with smoking, sits centrally and often originates in a bronchus, cavitates frequently, and causes cough, wheezing and hemoptysis once it obstructs the airway. Hypercalcemia is a paraneoplastic finding seen particularly with squamous cell carcinoma, and his calcium of 12.8 exceeds the 10.5 ceiling. — Pulmonary Nodules and Neoplasms 2026.pptx, Slides 14, 30, 31, 33, 39, 41, 46, 48"},{"q":"A 68-year-old man who is fit for surgery has an image-guided biopsy of a 22-mm spiculated (spiky) upper-lobe nodule that shows no malignancy. The nodule has enlarged on serial scans, and his team is concerned the biopsy missed the tumor. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Reassure and discharge","Annual screening scans","Chemotherapy now","Repeat biopsy or excision"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. When an image-guided biopsy does not confirm malignancy, the pathway is to consider a repeat biopsy or to proceed to excision if there is concern about a false-negative biopsy. His spiky border, upper-lobe location and growth over time are all concerning features, so a negative result does not end the workup. — Pulmonary Nodules and Neoplasms 2026.pptx, Slides 15, 16, 35"},{"q":"A 70-year-old man had chest radiotherapy for small cell lung carcinoma 4 years ago and remains free of cancer. He now has progressive exertional dyspnea, and computed tomography shows scarring confined to the treated area of lung. Which late effect of radiotherapy explains his symptoms?","choices":["Lymphedema","Neuropathy","Pulmonary fibrosis","Skin erythema"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Chronic effects of lung radiotherapy include pulmonary fibrosis and scarring of the affected tissues, as well as cardiovascular fibrosis, lymphedema and neuropathy. Scarring limited to the treated area years after radiation, with progressive exertional dyspnea, is radiation pulmonary fibrosis. — Pulmonary Nodules and Neoplasms 2026.pptx, Slide 45"},{"q":"A 63-year-old woman with a 35 pack-year history has a 9-mm solid nodule in the right upper lobe found on computed tomography for abdominal pain. Which additional feature of the nodule would most raise concern for malignancy?","choices":["Popcorn calcification","Internal fat","Central calcification","Spiculated border"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Concerning nodules are greater than 6 mm, sit in the upper lobes or hilar structures, have a spiculated appearance (spikes, deviations or lobulations in the border), number more than 3, enlarge over time, enhance with contrast, appear cavitated or nonsolid, or occur in high-risk patients. A spiky border would add to her size, location and smoking. — Pulmonary Nodules and Neoplasms 2026.pptx, Slides 16, 21"},{"q":"A 70-year-old woman with known small cell lung carcinoma has a serum sodium of 124 mEq/L (normal 135-145) and low serum osmolality from the syndrome of inappropriate antidiuretic hormone. Her family asks why her legs and lungs show no swelling if her body is holding on to too much water. Which response is accurate?","choices":["Her sodium is actually high","It develops slowly and insidiously","She must be dehydrated instead","Swelling will appear within days"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. In small cell lung cancer, neuroendocrine tumor cells produce excess antidiuretic hormone (vasopressin), and the body retains too much water, lowering serum sodium and osmolality. The result is confusion, seizures, ataxia and other neurologic dysfunction, but no signs of peripheral or pulmonary edema, because the process is insidious over time. — Pulmonary Nodules and Neoplasms 2026.pptx, Slide 48"},{"q":"A 66-year-old man with a 45 pack-year smoking history has a newly diagnosed squamous cell carcinoma forming a mass near the right hilum, with spread to the regional lymph nodes. He asks which distant organs the cancer is expected to spread to. Which response is accurate?","choices":["Liver, brain and bone","Small intestine and bowel","Heart and great vessels","Scalene and supraclavicular nodes"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Lung cancer proliferates locally, spreads to regional lymph nodes and then to distant organs, named as the liver, brain and bone, as angiogenesis lets it exceed its boundaries. Each site can announce itself without a respiratory symptom: a right upper quadrant ache, headaches or seizures, bone pain or fractures. — Pulmonary Nodules and Neoplasms 2026.pptx, Slides 9, 13, 22, 25, 33"},{"q":"A 61-year-old woman who has never smoked is diagnosed with lung cancer. For 30 years she has lived in a house whose basement tested high for radon, and she has no other known exposures. She asks how she could develop lung cancer without smoking. Which response is accurate?","choices":["Genetics is the sole cause","Radon exposure is a risk factor","Lung cancer requires smoking","Second-hand smoke explains it"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Smoking is implicated in 85 to 90% of lung cancers, not all of them, and radiation and radon exposure are listed among the other risk factors, beside air pollution, environmental and chemical toxins, tuberculosis or human immunodeficiency virus infection, genetics, diet and alcohol. Her 30 years of radon exposure is a recognized cause. — Pulmonary Nodules and Neoplasms 2026.pptx, Slide 3"},{"q":"A 34-year-old woman who has never smoked and has no other lung cancer risk factors has a new, solid 9-mm nodule seen on computed tomography done after a car crash. Her clinician judges the probability of malignancy to be very low. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Standard staging evaluation","Thoracoscopic resection","Surveillance computed tomography","No further imaging"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. For a new solid nodule of 8 to 30 mm, the first move is to judge the clinical probability of malignancy. A very low probability leads to computed tomography surveillance at 3 to 6, 9 to 12 and 18 to 24 months, using low-dose, thin-section, noncontrast scans, rather than staging, biopsy or resection. — Pulmonary Nodules and Neoplasms 2026.pptx, Slide 11"},{"q":"A 46-year-old woman who has never smoked has a small, flat nodular opacity lying along the minor fissure on computed tomography done after chest trauma. She asks whether it could be cancer. Which response is accurate?","choices":["Fissure location signals cancer","It needs removal now","It is a hamartoma","Likely scar or lymphoid tissue"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. A nodule close to a fissure usually indicates something benign, and a nodular opacity adjacent to the minor fissure is typical of a linear scar or lymphoid tissue. She is a never-smoker without other risk factors, which also lowers concern. — Pulmonary Nodules and Neoplasms 2026.pptx, Slides 16, 21"},{"q":"A 64-year-old woman with a 35 pack-year history has 2 months of persistent cough and hemoptysis. A chest film shows a 2-cm opacity in the right upper lobe. Which next study is least useful in her workup?","choices":["Metabolic panel with calcium","Repeat chest radiograph","Contrast chest computed tomography","Biopsy guided by nodule location"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. A chest film is not a good tool for working up a suspicious nodule or symptoms: it may be the first image to show a nodule, but otherwise it serves to rule out other pathology. Her symptoms and opacity call for computed tomography with contrast of the chest and upper abdomen, then biopsy, with laboratory studies. — Pulmonary Nodules and Neoplasms 2026.pptx, Slides 14, 46"},{"q":"A 63-year-old woman has a biopsy-proven 2-cm non-small cell carcinoma in the right upper lobe. On exam a firm right supraclavicular lymph node is palpable, and its biopsy shows the same cancer. Imaging shows no metastasis to other organs. Which stage is her cancer?","choices":["Stage 2B","Stage 3A","Stage 3B","Stage 4A"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Spread to any supraclavicular or scalene node is N3, which moves a cancer to stage 3. With a tumor no more than 5 cm, spread to supraclavicular or scalene nodes, or to mediastinal or hilar nodes on the side without the tumor, is stage 3B. Her 2-cm tumor with a positive supraclavicular node fits. — Pulmonary Nodules and Neoplasms 2026.pptx, Slides 22, 25, 27, 29"},{"q":"A 66-year-old man with a 3.5-cm non-small cell lung carcinoma, no lymph node involvement and no metastasis (stage 1B) is fit for surgery. He would prefer to avoid an operation and asks for chemotherapy and radiation instead. Which plan should be avoided as his primary treatment?","choices":["Chemoradiation without surgery","Lobectomy","Wedge or segment resection","Preoperative lung function tests"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Stage 1 non-small cell lung cancer is treated with surgical resection when the patient can tolerate it, and chemotherapy and radiation serve only as adjuncts to surgery or for stage 4, because non-small cell cancers respond poorly to them. For a fit patient with stage 1B disease, replacing surgery with chemoradiation gives up the standard treatment. — Pulmonary Nodules and Neoplasms 2026.pptx, Slides 14, 35"}]};
